# t1050 — `for c in s:` reads a TORN string when `s` is reassigned inside
# the loop: silent wrong output, exit 0, no diagnostic.
#
# `lower_for_string_with` snapshots the byte length ONCE before the loop,
# but makes the iterator an `emit_borrow` Ptr to `s`'s own slot and
# re-reads `->data` every iteration. Reassigning `s` swaps the buffer while
# the loop keeps walking to the OLD length — length and buffer disagree.
#
# Rust gg prints `aZZ` / `3`. ggdef prints `abc` / `3` and adjudicates
# definitively (Core #8): `aZZ` is not the answer under any semantics — the
# loop must iterate the string it started on.
#
# ⚠ THE CASE HAS NO SUBJECT (SIX Q#4). `E_MutationWhileBorrowed` guards
# exactly this class for collections, via
# `self.for_loop_iterables.contains(&recv_def_id)` in
# `src/semantic/safety/check_expr.rs` — but that check lives in the
# MUTATING-METHOD-CALL arm only. A plain REASSIGNMENT is the only way to
# mutate a String, and it reaches no rule at all. No widening of the
# existing rule's subject fixes this; the reassign route needs its own.
#
# SECOND-ORDER, same root: with a longer source the stale length walks off
# the end of the new buffer and reads freed/foreign heap. The two emissions
# of `gorget_str_codepoint_at` DISAGREE about that — `emit_types.rs` emits a
# helper WITH a bounds check and a cplen clamp, `emit_call_extern.rs` emits
# it INLINE with NEITHER, and the inline form is the one used.
#
# ⚠ ASan is blind (the custom `__gorget_current_alloc` pool). stdout is the
# instrument.
#
# INTENDED: `abc` then `3`.

void main():
    String s = "abc"
    String seen = ""
    int n = 0
    for c in s:
        seen = seen + c
        if n == 0:
            s = "ZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZZ"
        n = n + 1
    print(seen)
    print(n)
